arm: implement STRICT_KERNEL_RWX on PMSAv8

An MPU can do this, but the ARM port only offers ARCH_HAS_STRICT_KERNEL_RWX
when there is an MMU, so every NOMMU kernel with an MPU prints

  This architecture does not have kernel memory protection.

which on a machine whose MPU is enabled and enforcing is simply untrue -- what
is missing is not the hardware but the code to use it for this.

pmsav8_setup() maps the kernel as one region: readable, writable and executable
throughout. Splitting it in two costs one more region of the eight and gives the
usual guarantees -- text and rodata read-only, data and bss never executable.
The boundary is __init_begin rather than __end_rodata: everything below it is
text, rodata and the tables between them, and splitting lower would only move
those tables into the writable half for nothing.

The MPU is disabled across the change. Two regions have to be rewritten and
there is no way to do it in one step: shrinking the kernel region first leaves
data and bss unmapped and the next stack access faults, while writing the new
region first leaves the two overlapping, which is UNPREDICTABLE on ARMv8-M.
With the MPU off the default map applies and neither window exists.

Nothing here may be __init or use __initdata, because mark_rodata_ro() runs
after free_initmem(); the region count is read from MPUIR rather than from the
__initdata copy pmsav8_setup() kept.

Only PMSAv8 is implemented. On PMSAv7 mark_rodata_ro() says so, which is worth
more than a silent no-op that would leave the impression the kernel is
protected.

Measured on an RP2350 (Cortex-M33, 8 regions), the kernel region becomes:

  text+rodata  0x11008180-0x111e6000  PL1 RO, PL0 none, executable
  data+bss     0x111e6000-0x112cb000  PL1 RW, PL0 none, XN

six of eight regions used, and the message is gone because it is no longer true.

